    "result_text": "BUILD SUCCESS \u2014 all tests pass. Here's what I changed and why.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe householdId is now deterministic from `(lastName, postcode)`, and everything keying off the household follows it. I kept the impact contained to small, cohesive units so no existing method grew and no large class was disturbed.\n\n**`HouseholdId.java`** \u2014 the one behavioral core. `of()` now hashes `normalizedLastName + '|' + postcode` and takes the first **12** hex chars of SHA-256 (was 16 over lastName+address). Owners with the same last name and postcode now collapse to the same id automatically.\n\n**`RejectDuplicateOwnerHousehold.java`** (new, single method) \u2014 rejects **409** when another owner already shares the computed `householdId`. Setting `sharesHousehold` bypasses this block (early return), so the second owner is created as a *declared household member*. I reused the existing `DuplicateOwnerIdentityException` (already mapped to 409) rather than adding a new exception + handler + escalation YAML, keeping the file count down.\n\n**`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted `rejectDuplicateHousehold` into the pipeline right after the identity check.\n\n**`PossibleDuplicate.java`** \u2014 one guard at the top: a declared member (`sharesHousehold == true`) returns `null`, so it is not flagged as a suspected duplicate.\n\n**`HouseholdSize.java`** \u2014 the household-size input now groups owners by `HouseholdId.of(...)` instead of the old lastName+address comparison, so it tracks the same computed key.\n\n## Notes on the two \"duplicate\" concepts\n- The prior identity check (telephone + email) is untouched and still runs \u2014 it's a distinct concern from the household duplicate.\n- `sharesHousehold` previously did nothing on the create path; it now purely *bypasses the household block* (it never creates the link \u2014 the link is derived from the hash), matching the spec's changed behavior.\n\nTest result: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `coreSameLastNameAndPostcodeShareHouseholdId` passes (same lastName+postcode \u2192 equal householdId with the second owner created via `sharesHousehold`; different lastName \u2192 different householdId).\n\nOne thing worth flagging: `HouseholdSize` (and `MembershipTier`, which consumes it) remain **unwired** \u2014 no pipeline calls them, so the \"household-size input to the membership level\" change is spec-compliant but currently unobservable. `MembershipLevel` (the value actually placed on the DTO) is computed from email + namesake count, not household size. I left that wiring as-is since the spec only asked that the household-size input *use* the computed householdId, which it now does.",
